Rational Owned Choice: Finite Algebras and Sealed Rounds

The paper is main.tex, and its appendices form a technical report, tr.tex.
Below, "the paper" includes both. Select either one as the main document in
Overleaf and use pdfLaTeX, or build both locally with TeX Live and latexmk:

    make paper

The PDFs are build/main.pdf and build/tr.pdf. Each document reads the other's
numbering from a committed snapshot, sections/labels-main.tex or
sections/labels-tr.tex, so it builds alone. make paper refreshes these
snapshots; commit them after a change that renumbers anything. To create an
upload containing only the paper sources and bibliography:

    make overleaf

Upload build/overleaf.zip to Overleaf. The mathematical statements,
proofs, attribution, and proof-status inventory are all in the paper.

Known source refutation: Ghani--Kupke--Lambert--Nordvall Forsberg's
"Compositional Game Theory with Mixed Strategies" (arXiv:2009.06831v1)
has finite counterexamples to the unit law Id;K = K in Lemma 16
(a right unit in the source's circle notation) and associativity in
Theorem 15. Convex closing does not repair associativity.
The paper gives a self-contained proof in sections/appendix-open-games.tex,
"Finite obstructions to the proposed mixed open-game category". The
Lean proofs are pinned to GameTheory v1-final, revision
02898a2d8b918f9b106a683420ca78c99867560e, under
GameTheory/Languages/OpenGame/{Mixed,Examples}.lean:
https://github.com/elazarg/GameTheory/tree/02898a2d8b918f9b106a683420ca78c99867560e/GameTheory/Languages/OpenGame
This refutes the specified composition, not probabilistic open games
generally. The paper's closed-round SE recursion is proved separately.

Layout:

    main.tex          Paper entry point
    tr.tex            Technical report entry point, holding the appendices
    references.bib    Bibliography
    sections/         Sections, appendices, macros, and label snapshots
    scripts/          Label export between the two documents
    lean/             Twenty-two Lean proof modules, using only Std
    checks/           Python models and independent finite checks
    notes/            Exploration not claimed in the paper
    lean-toolchain    Lean version (4.34.1)
    build/            Generated PDF, upload ZIP, and compiled proofs

Run all verification with Python 3 and Lean installed through elan:

    make check

make check-python and make check-lean run either part separately. Python
needs only the standard library. Lean imports are built in dependency
order, with generated files under build/lean. make clean removes build/.
checks/probe_residual.py is an optional experiment, outside the checks;
it establishes no residual bound for the whole variety.
checks/probe_core_basis.py optionally enumerates small models of candidate
core laws, checks their entire two-variable theory, and tests owner
exhaustion and mixed absorption. The candidate without mixed absorption
has a six-state countermodel. Passing the extended search is not a
completeness proof.

checks/check_fragments.py independently evaluates the reset/union and
two-owner H hardness reductions, including every mixed valuation in its
stated finite cases. lean/Fragments.lean checks the semantic guard,
propagation, and separation certificates and the generic colouring
obstruction. The reduction-size and complexity arguments are in the paper.

checks/check_sealed_rounds.py compares exact rational Nash support rectangles
with unilateral deviations, checks the parallel-sum interpretation and rational
synthesis, and tests the infinite threshold family and nonconvexity witnesses.
lean/SealedRounds.lean proves ordered-field diagonal-stage certificates,
rational-generation arithmetic, and the impossibility of a finite summary of
all threshold probes. The SE round correspondence and the connection from
game syntax to those probes have written proofs in the paper.
checks/check_open_game_obstruction.py independently reproduces the
finite unit, nonassociativity, and failed convex-repair witnesses using
exact rational laws. It does not replace the pinned historical Lean proofs.

The paper distinguishes kernel-checked results, written generic proofs,
and finite checks. The dependent data-calculus metatheory and general
hidden-game composition are not certified by the algebraic Lean modules.

lean/FullAlgebra.lean proves the full-signature singleton-or-pair theorem,
sharp test-domain necessity and sufficiency, exact idempotent elimination,
equivalent core extraction, and preservation of player views by every
idempotent full-language term. It uses the existing FullTerm semantics,
including internal role permutations, with no added axioms.

lean/OwnerFragments.lean proves exact guard comparison and executable
checker correctness for reset with one H owner, including single-outsider
completeness for every K >= 3. It
also checks observational comparison with the observer's own H and one
other owner's H. lean/OwnerModels.lean proves four-element completeness
and the three-state obstruction used in the minimum-generator proof;
the order-algebra reduction uses a published theorem. checks/check_one_owner.py compares these tables with
the existing winner-set evaluator and constructs three-role separators.

sections/appendix-owner-synthesis.tex characterizes the realizable
one-owner guard tables and constructs native terms of size O(nM).
checks/check_owner_synthesis.py compares the algorithm with independent
four-state term closure through arity three and all 8000 root-fixed
four-input candidate tables. The synthesis theorem and clone substitution
formula have written proofs; they do not establish a finite identity basis.

sections/appendix-owner-basis.tex separately proves a finite basis over
arbitrary algebras for the one-owner signature {reset, H_a}. Its explicit
forty-equation basis uses at most six variables. Common-root word
normalization and two-source absorption give a written completeness proof.
lean/OwnerModels.lean checks soundness of all forty equations; it does not
formalize the generic completeness proof. checks/check_owner_finite_basis.py
independently checks native soundness, every product of the free
three-generated algebra, and larger sampled normalization and absorption
certificates. The minimum variable bound is between four and six.

Any one-owner equational basis needs at least four variables.
lean/OwnerModels.lean constructs a four-element countermodel, embeds every
proper subalgebra in F squared, and proves that every three-variable
identity transfers, with no term-size bound. A valid four-variable identity
fails in that model. This lower bound applies to the unrestricted basis.

For the K-owner core, every equational basis needs at least K+1 variables.
sections/appendix-core-basis.tex constructs a (K+2)-element algebra that
satisfies every K-variable identity but fails owner exhaustion. The same
section classifies all binary core terms: the free two-generated algebra
has 2^(K+1)+K+1 elements. The general proofs are written.
lean/CoreObstruction.lean kernel-checks the owner-exhaustion membership law
and, for three owners, the quotient maps, transfer of every three-variable
identity, and the four-variable failure. checks/check_core_obstruction.py
checks the quotients at K=3..6 and complete binary tables at K=3,4,5.
Whether the core has a finite basis remains open.

Unary polynomial functions, with arbitrary fixed parameters, also have an
exact normal form. Apart from constants, they preserve their input, add a
fixed set U on ambiguous inputs, and add an arbitrary subset of U minus i
at each singleton i. Every such profile is realizable. The exact count is
(1+2^(K-1))^K + 2^K - 2. Consequently every unary polynomial satisfies
p^3=p^2 throughout the core variety, and the exponent two is sharp.
checks/check_core_unary_polynomials.py verifies the entire 131-function
closure at K=3 and all 6575 synthesized functions at K=4. The normal form
classifies functions on the generator, not derivations from the candidate.

The same section gives a complete relative presentation for conservative
core models: 3+4K+binom(K,2) identities, with an optimal bound of three
variables. All but at most one owner operation equal derived union.
lean/CoreConservative.lean proves this structural lemma for arbitrary
carriers; completeness uses the cited dissemilattice generation theorem.
A written corollary makes the K+2-state witness above smallest possible:
for s >= 3, every algebra of size at most s+1 satisfying the entire
s-variable core theory already belongs to the core variety.

Every nonempty proper-owner fragment with m owners has an exact
(m+2)-state generator, also when union is retained. The union expansion
is term equivalent to the core subvariety in which unused owners equal
union; any finite core basis would yield a finite basis for that fragment.
The core variety is the join of these subvarieties with one unused owner.
The raw two-owner
reduct is the classical four-element bichain B; this observation does
not give a finite basis. lean/CoreMixed.lean proves a generic mixed
absorption identity and checks a six-state countermodel to the candidate
core laws even with owner exhaustion. checks/check_core_mixed.py checks
these certificates, all proper-owner maps at K=3,4,5, and the conservative
structural lemma on chains of sizes two through six.

Homomorphic images are essential in that variety join. A K+2-state
irreducible core model Q_K has every owner different from union, and cannot
embed into any product of proper-owner models. It is an explicit quotient
of a K+5-state subalgebra of two observer factors. lean/CoreQuotients.lean
proves the K=3 certificate, transfer of all identities at every arity,
and the monolith property. checks/check_core_quotients.py checks the generic
construction at K=3..8; its arbitrary-K proof is in the technical report.

The earlier six-state candidate countermodel is smallest possible.
checks/check_core_small_models.py exhaustively verifies that every model
of the candidate laws plus exhaustion on at most five elements is a core
model. It uses twenty explicit HSP certificates, each with at most thirteen
source elements, stored in checks/data/core-small-models.json. This finite
result does not prove an unrestricted basis.

After adding mixed absorption, the candidate is complete through eight
elements. The exhaustive verifications are run separately:

    python3 checks/probe_core_six_models.py
    python3 checks/probe_core_seven_models.py --size 8 --audit-six

The second command requires a C++17 compiler and uses a second semilattice
enumeration, audited against the original Python enumeration at size six.
Sizes six, seven, eight use 17, 46, 163 HSP certificates, with source sizes
at most 24, 47, 117. All certificates in checks/data/core-{six,seven,eight}-models.json
are checked by make check without a compiler. The remaining irreducibles
are conservative, one-owner dissemilattice expansions, or extensions by a
common absorbing element. Any countermodel to the strengthened candidate
has at least nine elements; unrestricted completeness remains unproved.

Observer tests can be reduced at every arity d to assignments with exactly
one upper marker. Recording only whether the output is that marker recovers
all outputs, including atoms. Across all K observers this uses d K^d bits;
full-valued evaluation on the same tests embeds the free d-generated core.
checks/check_core_sparse_tests.py independently reconstructs complete
sampled term tables from these bits.

For a separate family, membership is decided exactly by exhaustion:
incomparable owner atoms below an arbitrary chain of markers, with each
owner absorbing up to its own threshold. The technical report constructs
an explicit quotient of a subalgebra of the atomic quotient times proper
observer factors whenever the least threshold is zero, and proves the
corresponding criterion for infinite chains. checks/check_core_thresholds.py
verifies 441 instances of the construction and 295 exhaustion failures.
This excludes the whole threshold family as a source of countermodels
to the strengthened candidate; the full finite-basis question remains open.

Identifying all ambiguous winner sets gives a finitely based quotient over
arbitrary algebras. Its variety is exactly the core subvariety satisfying
x reset y = y. A sequential-veto term is near-unanimity of arity K+1;
the common-source theorem excludes smaller arities. Mitschke's and Baker's
external theorems then give congruence distributivity and a finite basis.
The proof establishes existence; it does not list that basis.
lean/CoreAtomic.lean proves generic reset comparison and the three-owner
veto certificate. checks/check_core_atomic.py checks the quotient at K=3..6
and near-unanimity at K=3..8. The original core has no near-unanimity term,
and its Q_K models show that reset commutation need not be transitive.

sections/appendix-basis.tex gives a finite presentation of all conservative
one-owner models: seventeen equations, reducible to thirteen in that class,
with an optimal bound of four variables. Every algebra with at most five
elements satisfying the entire four-variable theory is in the intended
variety; a countermodel to that theory must have at least six elements.
Its completeness proof uses forest thresholds and guard coverage, and is
written rather than formalized. lean/OwnerModels.lean checks the equations
and a nonconservative four-element countermodel showing that the seventeen
equations are not a full basis. It also checks a six-element subalgebra of
F squared mapping onto a nonconservative irreducible four-element model,
with collapse and pair-separation certificates for its monolith.
checks/check_owner_presentation.py independently checks the finite witnesses,
all 569 forest-based four-element candidates, and sampled guard thresholds.
Whether the core and full owned-choice signatures have finite bases remains open.

sections/appendix-core-basis.tex identifies raw owned choice with union as
the classical variety of meet-distributive bisemilattices, with a basis of
seven equations. The core contains expansions of that entire variety with
the same congruences. Its infinitely many irreducibles therefore rule out
a finite residual bound for the core; this does not decide whether a finite basis exists.
The generation and irreducible-family results are external (Harding and
Romanowska, Section 3.3). lean/CoreModels.lean proves the generic semantic
laws and the concrete three-state embedding; checks/check_core_basis.py
checks these with native winner sets at K=3,4,5.

sections/connections.tex gives private-store connection for fixed-schedule
disclosure fragments. It retains dependent witnesses, pending obligations,
audience views, full off-path strategies, and sealing certificates.
checks/check_connections.py enumerates finite profiles and fragment cuts.
The generic connection theorem has a written proof, not a Lean formalization
or a marginal-equilibrium composition claim.

Earlier drafts and tracked reviews are available in Git history through
69f763a. Uncommitted reviews are retained locally in .git/local-notes/.

lean/RawFragments.lean checks semantic certificates for all raw-pair
hardness reductions; checks/check_raw_pairs.py evaluates the actual native
reductions. Syntax assembly and complexity bounds have written proofs.

lean/ChainGuards.lean proves guard completeness and checker correctness for the native
two-owner G operation and, more generally, every one fixed nonempty
proper blocking policy. The same checker decides every such theory,
independently of K. checks/check_chain_guards.py evaluates the original
owned-choice expression and the policies independently. Polynomial size
bounds are the written guard-size argument in the paper.

sections/appendix-two-players.tex treats K=2, where the body of the paper's
assumption K>=3 is dropped. checks/check_two_players.py tests it by exact closure
and exact game values. Its results have written proofs and are not in Lean.

lean/MixedPolicies.lean proves union/intersection identities for blocking
policies, joint-test absorption, the abstract least-point coverage lemma,
and the observer-relative hardness certificates. Polynomiality for chains
and the complete two-policy complexity classification have written proofs;
checks/check_mixed_policies.py compares their guard algorithms with exact
winner-set tables and checks native hardness reductions.

The arbitrary-family classification is in
sections/appendix-policy-families.tex. Passing patterns give a complete
polynomial/coNP-complete dichotomy for every fixed finite policy family.
checks/check_policy_families.py tests the guard algorithm and expanded
native reductions; the generic classification has a written proof.

Shared reset circuits have coNP-complete equality even on the three-element
generator (sections/appendix-circuits.tex). lean/Fragments.lean proves
the clause and CNF marker recurrences. checks/check_reset_circuits.py
checks the actual DAG reduction, all mixed valuations in its finite cases,
the fixed-chain witnesses, and the linear gate count. Circuit syntax
assembly and the complexity theorem have written proofs.
